WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.178
Floorsight measures how close forklifts come to people,
slows or stops the forklift in time, and

2
00:00:03.228 --> 00:00:06.406
lets a person review.

3
00:00:07.067 --> 00:00:12.273
Forklifts and people share the same floor. Most
factories already record CCTV, but nobody

4
00:00:12.323 --> 00:00:17.529
watches it live, and a camera image cannot tell you how
far a person really is from a forklift.

5
00:00:18.179 --> 00:00:23.337
On screen, this seated operator seems to touch the
forklift. On the floor map they are about

6
00:00:23.387 --> 00:00:28.545
three metres away. A pixel rule would alarm all day.
Floorsight measures in metres.

7
00:00:29.212 --> 00:00:34.018
OpenCV 5's line-segment detector finds the floor's main
directions. Their vanishing points and

8
00:00:34.068 --> 00:00:38.874
two size references turn one ordinary camera into a
floor map in metres, with its error: the

9
00:00:38.924 --> 00:00:43.730
camera sits 3.56 metres up, plus or minus five percent.

10
00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:48.326
Every moving forklift carries its danger zone: where it
travels before it can stop, where its

11
00:00:48.376 --> 00:00:52.322
rear swings, and what a tall load hides, each drawn from
sixty-four sampled variations. If it

12
00:00:52.372 --> 00:00:56.318
will pass within 0.8 metres of a person in the next
three seconds on its course, Floorsight

13
00:00:56.368 --> 00:01:00.314
warns.

14
00:01:01.647 --> 00:01:05.683
The detectors redraw every outline from scratch, so the
raw zones jittered. A Kalman filter on

15
00:01:05.733 --> 00:01:09.769
every track, and a second one on the turn rate, now hold
them steady: the outline's jump between

16
00:01:09.819 --> 00:01:13.855
frames fell from about 43 centimetres to about 12, and
false turns that bent the zone toward

17
00:01:13.905 --> 00:01:17.941
people are gone.

18
00:01:21.413 --> 00:01:25.815
This is the app, on an AWS Graviton4 instance with the
Cloud Optimized OpenCV build. Six real

19
00:01:25.865 --> 00:01:30.267
clips replay as a live feed onto the floor map.

20
00:01:31.413 --> 00:01:36.051
When a warning is confirmed, the machine acts first:
Floorsight slows the forklift to walking

21
00:01:36.101 --> 00:01:40.739
pace, or stops it if the person is in its path. Then the
agent replays the last one and a half

22
00:01:40.789 --> 00:01:45.427
seconds, takes a close-up, measures again, and
recommends in one line.

23
00:01:50.413 --> 00:01:53.507
A person approves or rejects afterwards. Only approved
warnings reach the floor lead, through

24
00:01:53.557 --> 00:01:56.651
Amazon SNS.

25
00:01:57.301 --> 00:02:00.923
On the third warning, the forklift pulled away from a
stop in a new direction; the agent

26
00:02:00.973 --> 00:02:04.595
recognises that as real motion, not a tracking glitch.

27
00:02:07.301 --> 00:02:10.227
In this run: 3 warnings, each saved with every step to a
trace, and the morning report sums up

28
00:02:10.277 --> 00:02:13.203
the shift.

29
00:02:13.868 --> 00:02:18.750
Recorded video cannot show a forklift being stopped, so
we built a simulated warehouse whose

30
00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:23.682
forklift obeys Floorsight. Without the interlock, it
hits the picker who steps into its path.

31
00:02:26.001 --> 00:02:30.241
With it, the same vision pipeline sees the picker, the
forklift stops, waits until the picker is

32
00:02:30.291 --> 00:02:34.531
seen clear of its path, then drives on, slowing as it
passes. Both people who stepped into its

33
00:02:34.581 --> 00:02:38.821
path were passed without contact. It costs time: in
forty seconds it drove 27 metres instead of

34
00:02:38.871 --> 00:02:43.111
80.

35
00:02:46.668 --> 00:02:51.826
It is the same app and the same stop logic. Braking
cannot help someone who walks into the back

36
00:02:51.876 --> 00:02:57.034
of a forklift driving away, and nothing can stop for a
person no camera sees. We report both.

37
00:03:02.668 --> 00:03:07.538
Our hardest real case: on the twentieth of October the
forklift's outline swerved toward this

38
00:03:07.588 --> 00:03:12.458
operator in its last forty milliseconds, 0.67 metres,
and the warning came too late.

39
00:03:13.108 --> 00:03:18.080
Measured again with the publishable detectors and
smoothed tracks, the same clip never comes

40
00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:23.102
closer than 1.55 metres. Our earlier warnings here came
from a turn the new filter shows was

41
00:03:23.152 --> 00:03:28.124
noise. With one camera we cannot say which outline is
right; at that moment they disagree by

42
00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:33.146
about 1.2 metres, and no warning based on a forklift's
course can foresee a real swerve.

43
00:03:33.808 --> 00:03:38.486
On the same Graviton4 instance, the COOL build runs
adaptive threshold seven times as fast and

44
00:03:38.536 --> 00:03:43.214
one stream of our OpenCV chain ten percent faster, but
resize and four parallel processes are

45
00:03:43.264 --> 00:03:47.942
slower, and our whole perception stage, spent in neural
detectors, was three percent slower. We

46
00:03:47.992 --> 00:03:52.670
also reproduced a crash in COOL's polygon fill.

47
00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:59.222
Everything runs in one private AWS deployment, built
with Terraform: evidence in S3, track

48
00:03:59.272 --> 00:04:05.174
history in DynamoDB, alerts through SNS, metrics in
CloudWatch, and no open inbound port.

49
00:04:05.824 --> 00:04:10.118
Privacy runs first and fails closed: heads and faces are
pixelated before anything is stored or

50
00:04:10.168 --> 00:04:14.462
shown, covering 98 percent of the heads the earlier
detectors found.

